```
package main
import "fmt"
func main() {
    nums := []int{5, 9, 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 2, 7}
    for i := 0; i < len(nums); i++ {
        for j := i + 1; j < len(nums); j++ {
            if nums[i] < nums[j] {
                nums[i], nums[j] = nums[j], nums[i]
            }
        }
    }
    fmt.Println(nums)
}
```
In this implementation, we use a nested loop to compare each pair of elements in the slice. If the first element is less than the second element, we swap their positions. This process is repeated until the entire slice is sorted in descending order.

To analyze the grammatical function of a given sentence and classify it as a declarative sentence, you can use the Stanford Parser, which is a natural language processing tool that can analyze the syntactic structure of a sentence and identify its parts of speech, dependencies, and constituents.
Here's some sample Java code that uses the Stanford Parser to analyze a sentence and classify it as a declarative sentence:
```java
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.CoreAnnotations;
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.CoreLabel;
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.SentenceUtils;
import edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline.Annotation;
import edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline.StanfordCoreNLP;
import edu.stanford.nlp.semgraph.SemanticGraph;
import edu.stanford.nlp.semgraph.SemanticGraphCoreAnnotations;
import edu.stanford.nlp.trees.Tree;
import edu.stanford.nlp.trees.TreeCoreAnnotations;
import edu.stanford.nlp.util.CoreMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Properties;
public class SentenceAnalyzer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String sentence = "The cat sat on the mat.";
        // Set up the Stanford CoreNLP pipeline
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.setProperty("annotators", "tokenize, ssplit, pos, lemma, parse");
        StanfordCoreNLP pipeline = new StanfordCoreNLP(props);
        // Create an annotation for the sentence
        Annotation annotation = new Annotation(sentence);
        // Process the annotation using the pipeline
        pipeline.annotate(annotation);
        // Get the sentence as a list of CoreLabels
        List<CoreLabel> tokens = annotation.get(CoreAnnotations.TokensAnnotation.class);
        // Get the parse tree for the sentence
        Tree parseTree = annotation.get(TreeCoreAnnotations.TreeAnnotation.class);
        // Get the semantic graph for the sentence
        SemanticGraph semanticGraph = annotation.get(SemanticGraphCoreAnnotations.CollapsedCCProcessedDependenciesAnnotation.class);
        // Check if the sentence is declarative
        boolean isDeclarative = parseTree.firstChild().value().equals("S");
        // Get the subject and predicate of the sentence
        String subject = "";
        String predicate = "";
        if (isDeclarative) {
            Tree subjectTree = parseTree.firstChild().getChild(0);
            List<CoreLabel> subjectTokens = subjectTree.taggedLabeledYield();
            subject = SentenceUtils.listToString(subjectTokens);
            Tree predicateTree = parseTree.firstChild().getChild(1);
            List<CoreLabel> predicateTokens = predicateTree.taggedLabeledYield();
            predicate = SentenceUtils.listToString(predicateTokens);
        }
        // Print out the results
        System.out.println("Sentence: " + sentence);
        System.out.println("Is declarative: " + isDeclarative);
        System.out.println("Subject: " + subject);
        System.out.println("Predicate: " + predicate);
    }
}
```
This code takes a sentence as input, sets up the Stanford CoreNLP pipeline, creates an annotation for the sentence, processes the annotation using the pipeline, and then analyzes the parse tree and semantic graph to classify the sentence as declarative and extract its subject and predicate. The subject and predicate are obtained by traversing the parse tree and extracting the tokens corresponding to the subject and predicate constituents.
Note that this code assumes that the input sentence is well-formed and grammatically correct. If the sentence is malformed or contains errors, the Stanford Parser may not be able to analyze it correctly.

