Sure, here's an example of implementing a priority queue using the `PriorityQueue` class in Java with a maximum size limit:
```java
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;
public class MaxSizePriorityQueue<T> {
    private PriorityQueue<T> queue;
    private int maxSize;
    public MaxSizePriorityQueue(int maxSize, Comparator<? super T> comparator) {
        this.queue = new PriorityQueue<>(maxSize, comparator);
        this.maxSize = maxSize;
    }
    public boolean add(T element) {
        if (queue.size() < maxSize) {
            queue.offer(element);
            return true;
        } else if (queue.comparator().compare(element, queue.peek()) > 0) {
            queue.poll();
            queue.offer(element);
            return true;
        } else {
            return false;
        }
    }
    public T poll() {
        return queue.poll();
    }
    public T peek() {
        return queue.peek();
    }
}
```
In this implementation, the `MaxSizePriorityQueue` class takes in a maximum size limit and a `Comparator` object that is used to compare the elements in the queue. The `add()` method adds an element to the queue if the queue is not full, or if the element has a higher priority than the lowest-priority element in the queue. If the queue is full and the element has a higher priority, the lowest-priority element is removed and the new element is added.
The `compare()` method is used by the `PriorityQueue` class to determine the order of the elements in the queue. When a new element is added to the queue, the `compare()` method is called to compare the new element with the existing elements in the queue. In this implementation, the `Comparator` object passed to the constructor of `MaxSizePriorityQueue` is used to ensure that only elements with a certain property value are stored in the queue. The `compare()` method in the `Comparator` object should return a negative value if the first element has a lower priority than the second element, a positive value if the first element has a higher priority than the second element, or zero if the two elements have the same priority.

