In this example, the `Shape` class defines the `area()` and `perimeter()` methods, which are implemented differently in the `Circle` and `Rectangle` classes. We create an array of `Shape` pointers and assign `Circle` and `Rectangle` objects to them. When we call the `area()` and `perimeter()` methods on these objects through the `Shape` pointers, the appropriate implementation is called based on the type of object being pointed to. This demonstrates the ability of objects to take on multiple forms or exhibit different behaviors depending on the context in which they are used, which is the essence of polymorphism.

