        if (day >= STRIKE_START_DAY && day <= STRIKE_END_DAY)
        {
            strikeDays++;
            deliveriesPerDay = 0;
        }
        else
        {
            deliveriesPerDay = rand() % (MAX_DELIVERIES_PER_DAY - MIN_DELIVERIES_PER_DAY + 1) + MIN_DELIVERIES_PER_DAY;
        }
        int deliveries = deliveriesPerDay * DELIVERY_CAPACITY;
        if (strikeDays > 0)
        {
            cout << "Day " << day << ": Nationwide transportation strike ongoing.\n";
            strikeDayCounter++;
            if (strikeDayCounter == strikeDays)
            {
                cout << "Transportation strike ended.\n";
                strikeDayCounter = 0;
                strikeDays = 0;
            }
        }
        else
        {
            cout << "Day " << day << ": " << deliveries << " units delivered.\n";
            inventory += deliveries;
        }
        if (inventory > MAX_INVENTORY)
        {
            inventory = MAX_INVENTORY;
        }
        if (inventory < MIN_INVENTORY)
        {
            inventory = MIN_INVENTORY;
        }
        cout << "Inventory: " << inventory << " units.\n\n";
    }
    return 0;
}
```
This code simulates the supply chain of a small local grocery store over the course of a week. The store starts with half of its maximum inventory and receives deliveries based on a random number of deliveries per day (between 1 and 5) and a delivery capacity of 50 units per delivery. The code also simulates a nationwide transportation strike that lasts for three days (days 3 to 5), during which no deliveries are made. The strike ends on day 5.
The code outputs the number of units delivered each day, the current inventory level, and any strike-related messages. The inventory level is capped at the maximum and minimum inventory levels specified in the code.
