The theory of gravity, as proposed by Sir Isaac Newton, states that every object in the universe attracts every other object with a force that is directly proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them.
This can be mathematically represented using the following formula:
\begin{equation}
F = G \frac{m_1 m_2}{r^2}
\end{equation}
where F is the gravitational force between two objects with masses m1 and m2, separated by a distance r, and G is the gravitational constant.
The gravitational constant, denoted by G, is a fundamental constant of nature that appears in the formula for the gravitational force between two objects. Its value is approximately 6.674 x 10^-11 N m^2 / kg^2.
The significance of the gravitational constant lies in the fact that it relates the strength of the gravitational force to the masses of the objects and the distance between them. Without G, we would not be able to accurately calculate the gravitational force between two objects.
The units of G are N m^2 / kg^2, which can also be expressed as m^3 / kg s^2. This unit is derived from the formula for the gravitational force and reflects the fact that the force is proportional to the product of the masses, inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them, and is a force per unit mass.

